"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (and §{#}). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Where it is in the demo:</strong> the section Magnets and Oersted’s compass, in the demo Magnetic Fields of Currents (closed at first; open it).",
"<strong>Using the demo.</strong> Two bar magnets lie end to end, with compass needles around them showing the direction of the field. Gap between the magnets sets the space between them; Turn magnet B around swaps its poles; Cut magnet B in half splits it into two short magnets. Readouts say whether magnet B is attracted or repelled, and how strong the force is compared with a {#} cm gap. Oersted’s compass shows, from above, a compass needle under a wire that runs north and south. Current in the wire (positive flows north), Height of the wire above the needle and Earth’s horizontal field set the experiment; readouts give the wire’s field at the needle, the needle’s angle from north and \\(\\tan\\theta\\). Predict first hides the field and the angle, so you read the angle from the compass.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> Each bar magnet is two equal and opposite point poles at its ends, {#} cm apart (the Gilbert model), so the force readout is a ratio and its size is in arbitrary units. The wire is long and straight, with field \\(\\mu_0 I/(2\\pi h)\\) at the needle, horizontal and at right angles to the wire; the needle is lightly damped and settles along the sum of that field and Earth’s horizontal field. Simulated data.",
"See how magnets attract and repel and why there is no lone magnetic pole, then repeat Oersted’s discovery that an electric current turns a compass needle, and use the needle’s angle to measure the field of a current.",
"Every magnet has a north and a south pole. Like poles repel and unlike poles attract, and the force weakens quickly with distance. A compass needle is a small magnet: its north end points along the magnetic field, away from a north pole and toward a south pole. Cutting a magnet in half never isolates a pole; each piece has a north and a south pole of its own.",
"In {#} Oersted found that a current in a wire moves a compass needle. A long straight wire carrying current \\(I\\) makes a field",
"\\[B_{\\text{wire}} = \\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi h}\\]",
"at a distance \\(h\\), circling the wire (right hand rule), with \\(\\mu_0 = 1.25663706127\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{T\\cdot m/A}\\) (CODATA {#}, the value the demo uses; \\(4\\pi\\times10^{-7}\\) to nine figures, the value the textbook uses). With the wire running north and south just above the needle, its field at the needle points east or west, at right angles to Earth’s horizontal field \\(B_E\\). The needle settles along the sum of the two, at an angle \\(\\theta\\) from north with",
"\\[\\tan\\theta = \\frac{B_{\\text{wire}}}{B_E}.\\]",
